## webEnvironment Options

```java
// Default: mock servlet, no real server
@SpringBootTest(webEnvironment = SpringBootTest.WebEnvironment.MOCK)

// Real server, random port (most common for HTTP tests)
@SpringBootTest(webEnvironment = SpringBootTest.WebEnvironment.RANDOM_PORT)

// Real server, port from server.port property (risky for parallel tests)
@SpringBootTest(webEnvironment = SpringBootTest.WebEnvironment.DEFINED_PORT)

// No web environment — service/repository tests
@SpringBootTest(webEnvironment = SpringBootTest.WebEnvironment.NONE)
```

## HTTP Integration Test

```java
@SpringBootTest(webEnvironment = SpringBootTest.WebEnvironment.RANDOM_PORT)
class UserApiIntegrationTest {

    @LocalServerPort
    private int port;

    @Autowired
    private TestRestTemplate restTemplate;

    @Test
    void shouldCreateAndRetrieveUser() {
        // Create
        UserRequest request = new UserRequest("Alice", "alice@example.com");
        ResponseEntity<User> createResponse = restTemplate
            .postForEntity("/api/users", request, User.class);
        assertThat(createResponse.getStatusCode()).isEqualTo(HttpStatus.CREATED);

        // Retrieve
        Long id = createResponse.getBody().getId();
        ResponseEntity<User> getResponse = restTemplate
            .getForEntity("/api/users/" + id, User.class);
        assertThat(getResponse.getBody().getEmail()).isEqualTo("alice@example.com");
    }
}
```

## Property Overrides in Tests

```java
@SpringBootTest(properties = {
    "spring.jpa.hibernate.ddl-auto=create-drop",
    "app.feature.new-checkout=true"
})
```

## Context Caching

Spring caches the application context across test methods in the same JVM run. Tests with the same configuration share a context, making the test suite faster overall. Adding `@MockBean` or `@SpyBean` creates a new context — minimize their use for performance.

## Production Reality

A test suite with 500 `@SpringBootTest` tests is painful. Structure your tests in a pyramid: many fast unit tests, some `@WebMvcTest` / `@DataJpaTest` slice tests, few `@SpringBootTest` integration tests. The full-context tests should cover the wiring and end-to-end happy paths — not every business logic variation.

## What's Loaded

| Component | @WebMvcTest | @SpringBootTest |
|---|---|---|
| `@Controller` / `@RestController` | Yes | Yes |
| `@ControllerAdvice` | Yes | Yes |
| Filters | Yes | Yes |
| Spring Security | Yes | Yes |
| `@Service` | No — mock it | Yes |
| `@Repository` | No — mock it | Yes |
| `@Component` | No | Yes |
| `DataSource` | No | Yes |

## Basic Test

```java
@WebMvcTest(UserController.class)
class UserControllerTest {

    @Autowired
    private MockMvc mockMvc;

    @MockBean
    private UserService userService;  // Must mock — not loaded by slice

    @Test
    void shouldReturnUser() throws Exception {
        User user = new User(1L, "Alice", "alice@example.com");
        given(userService.findById(1L)).willReturn(Optional.of(user));

        mockMvc.perform(get("/api/users/1")
                .accept(MediaType.APPLICATION_JSON))
            .andExpect(status().isOk())
            .andExpect(jsonPath("$.name").value("Alice"))
            .andExpect(jsonPath("$.email").value("alice@example.com"));
    }

    @Test
    void shouldReturn404WhenUserNotFound() throws Exception {
        given(userService.findById(99L)).willReturn(Optional.empty());

        mockMvc.perform(get("/api/users/99"))
            .andExpect(status().isNotFound());
    }
}
```

## With Security

```java
@WebMvcTest(UserController.class)
class SecuredControllerTest {
    @Autowired
    private MockMvc mockMvc;

    @MockBean
    private UserService userService;

    @Test
    @WithMockUser(roles = "ADMIN")
    void adminCanAccessEndpoint() throws Exception {
        mockMvc.perform(get("/api/admin/users"))
            .andExpect(status().isOk());
    }

    @Test
    void unauthenticatedReturns401() throws Exception {
        mockMvc.perform(get("/api/admin/users"))
            .andExpect(status().isUnauthorized());
    }
}
```

## Production Reality

`@WebMvcTest` is your best tool for testing controller logic: input validation, response serialization, HTTP status codes, error handling. Don't test business logic here — mock the service layer and keep service logic in unit tests. This separation keeps each test focused and fast.

## What's Loaded

| Component | Loaded? |
|---|---|
| `@Repository` / JPA repos | Yes |
| `EntityManager` | Yes |
| `DataSource` (H2 in-memory) | Yes |
| Hibernate / JPA config | Yes |
| `@Service` | No |
| `@Controller` | No |
| Spring Security | No |

## Basic Test

```java
@DataJpaTest
class UserRepositoryTest {

    @Autowired
    private TestEntityManager entityManager;

    @Autowired
    private UserRepository userRepository;

    @Test
    void shouldFindUserByEmail() {
        // Setup: persist test data
        User user = new User("Alice", "alice@example.com");
        entityManager.persistAndFlush(user);

        // Exercise
        Optional<User> found = userRepository.findByEmail("alice@example.com");

        // Assert
        assertThat(found).isPresent();
        assertThat(found.get().getName()).isEqualTo("Alice");
    }

    @Test
    void shouldReturnEmptyWhenEmailNotFound() {
        Optional<User> found = userRepository.findByEmail("nobody@example.com");
        assertThat(found).isEmpty();
    }
}
```

## Testing Against Real Database

To use your actual database (PostgreSQL, MySQL) instead of H2:

```java
@DataJpaTest
@AutoConfigureTestDatabase(replace = AutoConfigureTestDatabase.Replace.NONE)
@ActiveProfiles("test")  // Points to test datasource config
class UserRepositoryIntegrationTest {
    // Tests run against real DB — use Testcontainers for true isolation
}
```

## With Testcontainers

```java
@DataJpaTest
@AutoConfigureTestDatabase(replace = AutoConfigureTestDatabase.Replace.NONE)
@Testcontainers
class UserRepositoryContainerTest {

    @Container
    static PostgreSQLContainer<?> postgres = new PostgreSQLContainer<>("postgres:15")
        .withDatabaseName("testdb");

    @DynamicPropertySource
    static void configureProperties(DynamicPropertyRegistry registry) {
        registry.add("spring.datasource.url", postgres::getJdbcUrl);
        registry.add("spring.datasource.username", postgres::getUsername);
        registry.add("spring.datasource.password", postgres::getPassword);
    }
}
```

## Production Reality

H2 in-memory is fine for testing JPQL queries and basic repository logic. It breaks down when you have database-specific features — PostgreSQL arrays, JSON columns, recursive CTEs, custom functions. For those cases, Testcontainers running a real PostgreSQL is far more reliable than wrestling with H2 compatibility.

## @MockBean

```java
@SpringBootTest
class OrderServiceTest {

    @Autowired
    private OrderService orderService;  // Real bean

    @MockBean
    private PaymentGateway paymentGateway;  // Mock — no real calls

    @MockBean
    private EmailService emailService;  // Mock

    @Test
    void shouldCompleteOrder() {
        Order order = new Order("item-1", 29.99);
        given(paymentGateway.charge(any(), anyDouble()))
            .willReturn(PaymentResult.success("txn-123"));

        Order completed = orderService.place(order);

        assertThat(completed.getStatus()).isEqualTo(OrderStatus.CONFIRMED);
        verify(emailService).sendConfirmation(completed);  // Verify called
    }
}
```

## @SpyBean

```java
@SpringBootTest
class AuditServiceTest {

    @Autowired
    private UserService userService;  // Real bean

    @SpyBean
    private AuditService auditService;  // Real bean, spied on

    @Test
    void shouldAuditUserCreation() {
        userService.createUser(new UserRequest("Alice", "alice@test.com"));

        // Verify real AuditService was called
        verify(auditService).record(eq(AuditEvent.USER_CREATED), any());
    }

    @Test
    void shouldHandleAuditFailure() {
        // Override one method to simulate failure
        doThrow(new AuditException("DB down"))
            .when(auditService).record(any(), any());

        // User creation should still succeed even if audit fails
        assertThatCode(() -> userService.createUser(new UserRequest("Bob", "bob@test.com")))
            .doesNotThrowAnyException();
    }
}
```

## @Mock vs @MockBean

```java
// @Mock — plain Mockito, NOT wired into Spring context
@ExtendWith(MockitoExtension.class)
class ServiceUnitTest {
    @Mock
    private UserRepository userRepository;

    @InjectMocks
    private UserService userService;
}

// @MockBean — Spring-aware mock, wired into context
@SpringBootTest
class ServiceIntegrationTest {
    @MockBean
    private UserRepository userRepository;

    @Autowired
    private UserService userService;  // Injected with mock repository
}
```

## Production Reality

Prefer `@Mock` with `@ExtendWith(MockitoExtension.class)` for unit tests — no Spring context needed, tests run in milliseconds. Reserve `@MockBean` for slice and integration tests where you genuinely need the Spring context but need to isolate one dependency.

## Setup

```java
@WebMvcTest(OrderController.class)
class OrderControllerTest {

    @Autowired
    private MockMvc mockMvc;

    @Autowired
    private ObjectMapper objectMapper;

    @MockBean
    private OrderService orderService;
}
```

## GET Request

```java
@Test
void shouldReturnOrder() throws Exception {
    Order order = new Order(1L, "pending", 99.99);
    given(orderService.findById(1L)).willReturn(Optional.of(order));

    mockMvc.perform(get("/api/orders/1")
            .accept(MediaType.APPLICATION_JSON))
        .andExpect(status().isOk())
        .andExpect(jsonPath("$.id").value(1))
        .andExpect(jsonPath("$.status").value("pending"))
        .andExpect(jsonPath("$.amount").value(99.99));
}
```

## POST Request with Body

```java
@Test
void shouldCreateOrder() throws Exception {
    OrderRequest request = new OrderRequest("item-1", 2, 49.99);
    Order created = new Order(5L, "created", 99.98);
    given(orderService.create(any())).willReturn(created);

    mockMvc.perform(post("/api/orders")
            .contentType(MediaType.APPLICATION_JSON)
            .content(objectMapper.writeValueAsString(request)))
        .andExpect(status().isCreated())
        .andExpect(header().string("Location", containsString("/api/orders/5")))
        .andExpect(jsonPath("$.id").value(5));
}
```

## Validation Error Test

```java
@Test
void shouldReturn400ForInvalidRequest() throws Exception {
    OrderRequest invalid = new OrderRequest(null, -1, 0);  // Multiple violations

    mockMvc.perform(post("/api/orders")
            .contentType(MediaType.APPLICATION_JSON)
            .content(objectMapper.writeValueAsString(invalid)))
        .andExpect(status().isBadRequest())
        .andExpect(jsonPath("$.errors").isArray())
        .andExpect(jsonPath("$.errors", hasSize(greaterThanOrEqualTo(2))));
}
```

## Printing Response for Debugging

```java
mockMvc.perform(get("/api/orders/1"))
    .andDo(print())  // Prints full request/response to console
    .andExpect(status().isOk());
```

## Production Reality

MockMvc tests are the backbone of controller testing. They're fast enough to run on every commit and thorough enough to catch serialisation issues, missing fields, wrong status codes, and validation logic. Write one MockMvc test per controller method covering the happy path, the not-found case, and at least one validation error case.

## Setup

```java
@SpringBootTest(webEnvironment = SpringBootTest.WebEnvironment.RANDOM_PORT)
class OrderApiIntegrationTest {

    @Autowired
    private TestRestTemplate restTemplate;

    @LocalServerPort
    private int port;  // Not needed with TestRestTemplate — it auto-uses the port
}
```

## GET Request

```java
@Test
void shouldReturnOrder() {
    ResponseEntity<Order> response = restTemplate
        .getForEntity("/api/orders/1", Order.class);

    assertThat(response.getStatusCode()).isEqualTo(HttpStatus.OK);
    assertThat(response.getBody()).isNotNull();
    assertThat(response.getBody().getId()).isEqualTo(1L);
}
```

## POST Request

```java
@Test
void shouldCreateOrder() {
    OrderRequest request = new OrderRequest("item-1", 2, 49.99);

    ResponseEntity<Order> response = restTemplate
        .postForEntity("/api/orders", request, Order.class);

    assertThat(response.getStatusCode()).isEqualTo(HttpStatus.CREATED);
    assertThat(response.getHeaders().getLocation()).isNotNull();
}
```

## Authenticated Requests

```java
@Test
void shouldRequireAuthentication() {
    ResponseEntity<String> response = restTemplate
        .getForEntity("/api/protected", String.class);
    assertThat(response.getStatusCode()).isEqualTo(HttpStatus.UNAUTHORIZED);
}

@Test
void shouldSucceedWithCredentials() {
    TestRestTemplate authed = restTemplate.withBasicAuth("user", "password");
    ResponseEntity<String> response = authed
        .getForEntity("/api/protected", String.class);
    assertThat(response.getStatusCode()).isEqualTo(HttpStatus.OK);
}
```

## MockMvc vs TestRestTemplate

| | MockMvc | TestRestTemplate |
|---|---|---|
| Real HTTP? | No (in-process) | Yes |
| Server needed? | No | Yes (RANDOM_PORT) |
| Speed | Faster | Slower |
| Exception on 4xx? | Depends | No (fault-tolerant) |
| Use with | `@WebMvcTest`, `@SpringBootTest(MOCK)` | `@SpringBootTest(RANDOM_PORT)` |

## Production Reality

For API contract testing — verifying the exact JSON shape, headers, and status codes your API produces — `TestRestTemplate` is definitive. Use it for a small set of end-to-end contract tests and MockMvc for the broader test suite. The MockMvc suite runs in seconds; the `TestRestTemplate` suite in minutes.

