## PostgreSQL Integration Test

```java
@SpringBootTest
@Testcontainers
@AutoConfigureTestDatabase(replace = AutoConfigureTestDatabase.Replace.NONE)
class UserRepositoryTest {

    @Container
    static PostgreSQLContainer<?> postgres = new PostgreSQLContainer<>("postgres:15-alpine")
        .withDatabaseName("testdb")
        .withUsername("testuser")
        .withPassword("testpass");

    @DynamicPropertySource
    static void configureProperties(DynamicPropertyRegistry registry) {
        registry.add("spring.datasource.url", postgres::getJdbcUrl);
        registry.add("spring.datasource.username", postgres::getUsername);
        registry.add("spring.datasource.password", postgres::getPassword);
    }

    @Autowired
    private UserRepository userRepository;

    @Test
    void shouldPersistUser() {
        User user = new User("Alice", "alice@test.com");
        User saved = userRepository.save(user);

        Optional<User> found = userRepository.findByEmail("alice@test.com");
        assertThat(found).isPresent();
        assertThat(found.get().getId()).isEqualTo(saved.getId());
    }
}
```

## Multiple Containers

```java
@Container
static PostgreSQLContainer<?> postgres = new PostgreSQLContainer<>("postgres:15");

@Container
static GenericContainer<?> redis = new GenericContainer<>("redis:7-alpine")
    .withExposedPorts(6379);

@DynamicPropertySource
static void properties(DynamicPropertyRegistry registry) {
    registry.add("spring.datasource.url", postgres::getJdbcUrl);
    registry.add("spring.redis.host", redis::getHost);
    registry.add("spring.redis.port", () -> redis.getMappedPort(6379));
}
```

## Production Reality

Testcontainers requires Docker available on the test machine. In CI, this is standard — GitHub Actions, GitLab CI, and Jenkins all support Docker. The 2-3 second container startup cost is worth it for tests that accurately reflect production behaviour. H2 compatibility issues with PostgreSQL-specific features — JSON columns, array types, sequences — are eliminated entirely.

## Common Test Slices

| Annotation | Loads | Use For |
|---|---|---|
| `@WebMvcTest` | Controllers, security, filters | HTTP layer tests |
| `@DataJpaTest` | Repositories, JPA, DataSource | Persistence tests |
| `@JsonTest` | Jackson ObjectMapper, serializers | JSON serialization tests |
| `@RestClientTest` | `RestTemplate`, `@Service` with HTTP | Outbound HTTP client tests |
| `@DataMongoTest` | MongoDB repositories | MongoDB persistence tests |
| `@DataRedisTest` | Redis repositories | Redis persistence tests |
| `@WebFluxTest` | WebFlux controllers | Reactive HTTP layer tests |

## @JsonTest Example

```java
@JsonTest
class OrderSerializationTest {

    @Autowired
    private JacksonTester<Order> json;

    @Test
    void shouldSerializeOrder() throws Exception {
        Order order = new Order(1L, "pending", BigDecimal.valueOf(99.99));

        assertThat(json.write(order)).hasJsonPathValue("$.id", 1);
        assertThat(json.write(order)).hasJsonPathValue("$.status", "pending");
        assertThat(json.write(order)).doesNotHaveJsonPathValue("$.internalField");
    }

    @Test
    void shouldDeserializeOrder() throws Exception {
        String content = "{\"id\": 1, \"status\": \"pending\", \"amount\": 99.99}";

        Order order = json.parseObject(content);
        assertThat(order.getId()).isEqualTo(1L);
    }
}
```

## @RestClientTest Example

```java
@RestClientTest(PaymentServiceClient.class)
class PaymentServiceClientTest {

    @Autowired
    private PaymentServiceClient client;

    @Autowired
    private MockRestServiceServer mockServer;  // Auto-configured

    @Test
    void shouldCallPaymentApi() {
        mockServer.expect(requestTo("/api/pay"))
            .andExpect(method(HttpMethod.POST))
            .andRespond(withSuccess("{\"status\": \"approved\"}", MediaType.APPLICATION_JSON));

        PaymentResult result = client.pay(new PaymentRequest("order-1", 99.99));
        assertThat(result.getStatus()).isEqualTo("approved");
    }
}
```

## Production Reality

A well-structured test suite uses all three levels: unit tests (plain JUnit + Mockito) for business logic, slice tests for layer-specific concerns, and full integration tests for critical end-to-end paths. The right mix reduces total test run time while maximising coverage and confidence.

