## Priority Order (Highest to Lowest)

1. `@DynamicPropertySource`
2. `@SpringBootTest(properties = ...)` inline
3. `@TestPropertySource(properties = ...)` inline
4. `@TestPropertySource(locations = ...)` file
5. `application-test.properties` (with active `test` profile)
6. `application.properties`

## Production Reality

Keep a single `application-test.properties` for shared test infrastructure settings and use `@SpringBootTest(properties=...)` only for test-specific overrides. Scattering properties across multiple mechanisms in the same test class makes it hard to understand which value is actually active.

## Default Rollback

```java
@DataJpaTest  // Implicitly @Transactional
class UserRepositoryTest {

    @Autowired
    private UserRepository repository;

    @Test
    void testOne() {
        repository.save(new User("Alice", "alice@test.com"));
        assertThat(repository.count()).isEqualTo(1);
        // Transaction rolls back -> DB is clean for next test
    }

    @Test
    void testTwo() {
        // Alice from testOne is gone — clean state
        assertThat(repository.count()).isEqualTo(0);
    }
}
```

## Disable Rollback

```java
@Test
@Commit  // Or @Rollback(false)
void shouldPersistForDebugging() {
    repository.save(new User("Bob", "bob@test.com"));
    // Transaction commits — useful for debugging, but must clean up manually
}
```

## The REQUIRES_NEW Caveat

```java
// Production code
@Transactional(propagation = Propagation.REQUIRES_NEW)
public void auditLog(String event) {
    auditRepo.save(new AuditEntry(event));
    // This COMMITS independently — not rolled back by test's outer transaction
}

// Test problem: audit entry persists even though test's outer transaction rolls back
@Test
@Transactional
void testAuditLog() {
    service.doSomething();  // Internally calls auditLog with REQUIRES_NEW
    // Outer test rolls back, but audit entry is committed — leaks to next test!
}
```

**Fix:** Use `@Sql(scripts = "cleanup.sql", executionPhase = AFTER_TEST_METHOD)` to clean up REQUIRES_NEW data.

## Production Reality

Test transactions with rollback work well for most persistence tests. The edge case to watch is `REQUIRES_NEW` propagation — commonly seen in audit logging and error recording services that must commit regardless of the caller's transaction outcome. For those scenarios, use Testcontainers with explicit cleanup scripts instead of relying on rollback.
